1
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:08,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Is love the path to happiness?

2
00:00:10,974 --> 00:00:12,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Is love the path to happiness?

3
00:00:13,236 --> 00:00:15,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Loving your family, loving your dogs, loving...

4
00:00:15,938 --> 00:00:19,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just nature, the nature of love in general.

5
00:00:19,461 --> 00:00:19,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

6
00:00:19,842 --> 00:00:21,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that bring happiness?

7
00:00:21,763 --> 00:00:21,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

8
00:00:22,524 --> 00:00:23,345
[SPEAKER_00]: At the end of the day?

9
00:00:24,085 --> 00:00:25,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the short answer is absolutely not.

10
00:00:27,728 --> 00:00:33,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the common phenomenon of love, it's inseparable from attachment.

11
00:00:36,035 --> 00:00:37,956
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not attached to something, you're not loving it.

12
00:00:38,696 --> 00:00:43,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole notion of, you know, the unconditional love, well, it's a contradiction in terms.

13
00:00:45,439 --> 00:00:53,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either unconditionally bound with it, or you're emotionally divorced from it, which means you don't love it, which means you're not attached to it.

14
00:00:56,443 --> 00:01:00,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in itself, love is a bond.

15
00:01:01,407 --> 00:01:11,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Even you can make it as abstract and divine as you want, it's still a bond, because it still has its object, which means there is still some sort of attachment, to a degree.

16
00:01:15,492 --> 00:01:26,038
[SPEAKER_01]: But, actually, like, that's just people's fantasy, very often, when they talk about the unconditional love and, like, an ideal, an abstract ideal, that, in practice, love is, it's, it's even worse.

17
00:01:26,398 --> 00:01:35,243
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, it's, it's always, in fact, it's always mixed with infatuation, with lust, with delight, with conceit, with pride, with everything else.

18
00:01:35,303 --> 00:01:37,184
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, basically, what love is.

19
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:37,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Passion.

20
00:01:37,884 --> 00:01:38,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Passion, of course.

21
00:01:38,805 --> 00:01:39,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Inseparable from passion.

22
00:01:41,486 --> 00:01:44,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It's where you can kill, at the same time.

23
00:01:44,387 --> 00:01:45,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Fall forward.

24
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:48,288
[SPEAKER_00]: You can kill for it.

25
00:01:48,468 --> 00:01:53,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm sure that people who try to justify the love, they would think that.

26
00:01:59,793 --> 00:02:05,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, trying to be unconditioned, have unconditioned love for everything.

27
00:02:06,356 --> 00:02:10,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is just an ideal, basically, that you can't... that's a contradiction in terms.

28
00:02:11,839 --> 00:02:12,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're

29
00:02:14,005 --> 00:02:18,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, love, be it even unconditional, is a relationship.

30
00:02:21,849 --> 00:02:26,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you are relating yourself to these things, which means to that extent you're bound by them.

31
00:02:31,854 --> 00:02:33,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's impossible to have unconditional love.

32
00:02:34,562 --> 00:02:38,805
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's unconditioned, means there is no condition, there is no relationship.

33
00:02:39,465 --> 00:02:40,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But love is a relationship.

34
00:02:40,726 --> 00:02:52,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically saying like, oh, eh... the unconditional conditioning, or the unconditionally conditioned, that's what unconditional love would be.

35
00:02:52,354 --> 00:02:54,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Or have a relationship with no relationship.

36
00:02:55,216 --> 00:02:56,938
[SPEAKER_01]: That just... that's a contradiction in terms.

37
00:02:57,883 --> 00:03:07,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can find that poetic value in saying contradictory terms, but in itself, in practice and phenomenologically speaking, makes absolutely no sense.

38
00:03:09,553 --> 00:03:13,176
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either bound or you're free from the bound... from the... from the bond.

39
00:03:14,377 --> 00:03:16,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is love virtuous?

40
00:03:17,799 --> 00:03:18,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it a virtue?

41
00:03:20,742 --> 00:03:24,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends, but no, no.

42
00:03:25,786 --> 00:03:26,987
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, love can be wholesome,

43
00:03:28,643 --> 00:03:31,265
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one sort of slow in answering.

44
00:03:32,326 --> 00:03:40,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Love can be wholesome in a sense of... it's not necessarily, you know, infatuated with passion and lust and greed involved with it.

45
00:03:41,514 --> 00:03:51,482
[SPEAKER_01]: But as a bond, as something that is fundamentally still rooted in a degree of passion and emotion, it is unwholesome.

46
00:03:51,522 --> 00:03:54,585
[SPEAKER_01]: From the point of view of enlightenment, it is unwholesome.

47
00:03:55,706 --> 00:03:56,907
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an obstacle, basically.

48
00:03:58,544 --> 00:03:59,985
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bond that you need to break.

49
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:04,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Love or hate, basically.

50
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:11,853
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to be free from suffering, or if you want to develop your mind to the... well, it's full potential, so to speak.

51
00:04:11,913 --> 00:04:12,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Potential freedom.

52
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:15,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Both must be abandoned equally.

53
00:04:17,436 --> 00:04:18,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Both love and hate.

54
00:04:21,899 --> 00:04:24,621
[SPEAKER_00]: So, love cannot bring happiness?

55
00:04:24,981 --> 00:04:26,442
[SPEAKER_01]: It can bring worldly happiness.

56
00:04:27,655 --> 00:04:30,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, from the ultimate point of view, it's not happiness, obviously.

